  "summary": "Deep Fear is System Sacom and Sega's survival horror game, first released for Saturn in Japan on 16 July 1998.",
  "body": [
    "Deep Fear is System Sacom and Sega's survival horror game, first released for Saturn in Japan on 16 July 1998. John Mayor explores the Big Table, an underwater research facility in the Pacific where an infection transforms staff into hostile creatures. Finding survivors and mission items carries him between sections of the base, while weapons and medical supplies help him withstand the threat. Fixed views of pre-rendered environments frame the three-dimensional characters. The setting gives the familiar search through dangerous rooms another pressure: every part of the station depends on a limited supply of air.",
    "Air in each area decreases over time, and gunfire consumes it more quickly. Air System terminals restore the supply and also serve as save points, making them important destinations when planning a route. Underwater passages require a breathing mask with its own limited reserve. Fighting therefore spends more than ammunition: staying in an encounter can also reduce the time available to escape it. Weapon lockers provide equipment and restocking opportunities, while a separate Special weapon slot allows one powerful additional option to accompany John's regular guns.",
    "The production combines monster designs by Yasushi Nirasawa with music by Kenji Kawai, giving the base's failing systems and transformed inhabitants a strong science-fiction character. Rieko Kodama was among its producers. Exploring the station means tracing practical links between people, equipment and rooms while the infection gradually undermines them. A safe terminal or stocked locker becomes reassuring because it supports the next expedition. The underwater setting makes that dependence tangible: the facility offers shelter from the ocean, yet its own dwindling air and infected occupants make remaining inside increasingly dangerous."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Something inside the research station",
    "The air is another resource",
    "Horror beneath the surface"
  ],
